statement_as_printed:
Let $\psi_m$ be the standard division polynomial of a smooth
Weierstrass model, normalized by $\psi_1=1$, $\psi_2=2y$.
For $m\ge2$ and $Q,mQ\ne O$,
\begin{equation}\label{ari:eq:cs-division}
 \mathcal N(Q)^{m^2}
       =\mathcal N(mQ)\frac{|\Delta_E|^{(m^2-1)/12}}{|\psi_m(Q)|}.
\end{equation}
For the displayed family this specializes to
\[
 \mathcal N(P^{\mathrm{div}})^4=3^{3/4}|t-1|^{-1/4}\mathcal N(2P^{\mathrm{div}}),\qquad
 \mathcal N(P^{\mathrm{div}})^9=16|t|^{-2/3}\mathcal N(3P^{\mathrm{div}}).
\]
If $P^{\mathrm{div}}_t$ has exact order $n$ and $m\ge2$ satisfies
$m\equiv1$ or $-1\pmod n$, then
\begin{equation}\label{ari:eq:cs-torsionvalue}
 \mathcal N(P^{\mathrm{div}}_t)^{12}
       =\frac{|\Delta_{\mathrm{div}}(t)|}{|\psi_m(P^{\mathrm{div}}_t)|^{12/(m^2-1)}}.
\end{equation}
The positive value on the right is independent of $m$.

On a local period chart write $\mu=a\tau+b$, with $a,b$ real,
and put $T=\Im\tau$. The positive Betti measure is
\[
 m_{P^{\mathrm{div}}}=db\wedge da
 =\frac{|\mu_{z_B}-a\tau_{z_B}|^2}{T}\,dA_{z_B}.
\]
Thus the form $da\wedge db$ in the source request labelled Lead 15(b) has the wrong sign.
The globally single-valued function $\mathcal L(t)=\log \mathcal N(P^{\mathrm{div}}_t)$ satisfies
on $\mathbb{P}^1(\C)$
\begin{equation}\label{ari:eq:ca-current}
 dd^c\mathcal L=\frac1{12}\delta_\infty-m_{P^{\mathrm{div}}},\qquad
               \int_{\mathbb{P}^1}m_{P^{\mathrm{div}}}=\frac1{12}.
\end{equation}
There are no atoms of $m_{P^{\mathrm{div}}}$ at $0$, $1$ or $\infty$.
Let $T_0=\sqrt3/2$, $\rho=(1+i\sqrt3)/2$, and
$p=\wp'_\rho((2-\rho)/3)$. The density in the ordinary
$t$ coordinate satisfies
\begin{align}
 \frac{dm_{P^{\mathrm{div}}}}{dA_t}
 &=\frac{|t|^{-2/3}}{64T_0|p|^{2/3}}
                      \bigl(1+O(|t|^{1/3})\bigr),
                         &&t\longrightarrow0,\label{ari:eq:ca-density0}\\
 \frac{dm_{P^{\mathrm{div}}}}{dA_t}
 &=\frac{|t-1|^{-3/2}}{24\pi^2\sqrt{E_4(i)}}
                      \bigl(1+O(|t-1|^{1/2})\bigr),
                         &&t\longrightarrow1.\label{ari:eq:ca-density1}
\end{align}

The theta formula gives the source identity (FAM C.2.1, equation~\texttt{cd-norm})
\begin{equation}\label{fam:eq:cd-norm}
D_{\rm part}=\frac6\pi\log\frac{\mathcal N(\mu,\tau)}{2\sqrt3}.
\end{equation}

Both $\mathcal A$ and $\mathcal E_{\rm cot}$ are locally free, of ranks three and
two.  The vertical vector $\xi=\partial/\partial\zeta_2$ is nowhere
zero as a section of $\mathcal E_{\rm cot}^\vee$.  There is an exact sequence
of holomorphic vector bundles
\begin{equation}\label{inv:eq:A-E-line}
0\longrightarrow N_{\rm det}\longrightarrow\mathcal E_{\rm cot}
 \xrightarrow{\langle\cdot,\xi\rangle}\mathcal O_X\longrightarrow0,
\qquad N_{\rm det}=\det\mathcal E_{\rm cot}=\omega_f(-2S_1-3S_2).
\end{equation}

For every admissible $c$, the six direct images are
\begin{equation}\label{inv:eq:A-absolute-table}
\begin{array}{c|cc}
q&R^qf_*\Omega_X^1&R^qf_*\Omega_X^2\\\hline
0&\mathcal O(-2)&\mathcal O(-2)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)\\
1&\mathcal O\oplus\mathcal O(-1)^2&\mathcal O(-2)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)^2\\
2&\mathcal O\oplus\mathcal O(-1)&\mathcal O.
\end{array}
\end{equation}
In particular every listed sheaf is torsion-free.

For $n\in\mathbb Z$ define
\[
 r_3(n)\equiv n\pmod3,\quad 0\le r_3(n)<3,
 \qquad r_4(n)\equiv3n\pmod4,\quad0\le r_4(n)<4,
\]
\begin{gather*}
 d(n)=\frac{n-4r_3(n)-3r_4(n)}{12},\qquad
 \epsilon_n=\begin{cases}0&3\mid n,\\1&3\nmid n.\end{cases}
\\
 u(n)=\max\{d(n)+1,0\},\qquad
 v(n)=\max\{-d(n)-1,0\}.
\end{gather*}
The coarse direct images of $T_X\otimes M_n$ are
\begin{align*}
 f_*(T_X\otimes M_n)&=\mathcal O(d(n))\oplus\mathcal O(d(n-1)),\\
 R^1f_*(T_X\otimes M_n)&=\mathcal O(d(n))\oplus\mathcal O(d(n-1))\oplus\mathcal O(d(n+2)),\\
 R^2f_*(T_X\otimes M_n)&=\mathcal O(d(n+6))\oplus\mathbb C_{p_1}^{\epsilon_n}.\tag{C.5}
\end{align*}
Their global cohomology dimensions are
\begin{align*}
 h^0&=u(n)+u(n-1),\\
 h^1&=v(n)+v(n-1)+u(n)+u(n-1)+u(n+2),\\
 h^2&=v(n)+v(n-1)+v(n+2)+u(n+6)+\epsilon_n,\\
 h^3&=v(n+6).                                             \tag{C.6}
\end{align*}
The natural map $H^2(X,T_X\otimes M_n)\to H^2(\mathrm dP_6,K_{\mathrm dP_6})=\mathbb C$ has rank one exactly for
\[
 n\in\{-6,-3,-2\}\cup\mathbb Z_{\ge0};                         \tag{C.7}
\]
it is zero for all other integers. In particular $M_{-1}$ has tangent cohomology $(0,0,1,0)$ and a zero map to the normalization target.
